question:
Alexio has $100$ cards numbered $1$ to $100$ and chooses one at random.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] What is the probability that the number on the card is a multiple of $2$, $3$ or $5$ (at least one of them)?
\item[(b)] What is the probability that it is a multiple of all three of $2$, $3$ and $5$?
\end{enumerate}

The digits $1,2,3,\dots,9$ are placed, one per circle, in the nine circles of the triangle below (four circles on each side, the corner circles belonging to two sides), so that the sums of the four numbers on the three sides are all equal.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(3.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] P={(0,0),(6,0),(3,5.196)};
pair[] Q={P[0]+(P[1]-P[0])/3,P[0]+2*(P[1]-P[0])/3,P[1]+(P[2]-P[1])/3,P[1]+2*(P[2]-P[1])/3,P[2]+(P[0]-P[2])/3,P[2]+2*(P[0]-P[2])/3};
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--cycle,gray+linewidth(0.6));
for(int i=0;i<3;++i) filldraw(circle(P[i],0.55),white,linewidth(0.8));
for(int i=0;i<6;++i) filldraw(circle(Q[i],0.55),white,linewidth(0.8));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

What is the positive difference between the greatest and the least possible common sum?

solution:
Let the common side sum be $T$ and let $c$ be the sum of the three corner numbers. Adding the three side sums counts every number once and the corners twice:
\[
3T=(1+2+\dots+9)+c=45+c .
\]
The corners are three different digits, so $1+2+3=6\le c\le7+8+9=24$, and therefore $17\le T\le23$.

Both extremes occur:

